{
  "teamId": 147,
  "year": 2020,
  "narrative": "The 2020 New York Yankees entered baseball's pandemic-shortened 60-game sprint with championship expectations and a roster built to dominate. Their July was a glimpse of the ceiling — a 5-1 start powered by Aaron Judge's early explosiveness, including a three-run blast against Baltimore that erased a damaging Pedro Severino home run and set the tone for what the Yankees hoped would be a wire-to-wire statement season.\n\nAugust tested their resolve. The Yankees went 14-13, a treading-water month defined by bullpen volatility and late-game heartbreak. Aroldis Chapman, their elite closer, surrendered crushing home runs that exposed a fragility behind the marquee names. Yet the month wasn't without its gems — a remarkable August 30th saw Aaron Hicks, Gio Urshela, and Gary Sánchez combine for jaw-dropping moments, including a Sánchez grand slam, in what became one of the season's most memorable individual days at the plate.\n\nSeptember brought more of the same — a 14-13 record that mirrored August's inconsistency. Chapman was victimized again, this time by J.D. Davis, and double plays at critical moments squandered rally after rally. But Gary Sánchez continued to flash power, and the Yankees survived the grind to secure a playoff spot, finishing 33-27 in a season where the margin for error was razor-thin and every game carried postseason weight.\n\nThe October story was a tale of two wild days and a series too far. Against Cleveland in the Wild Card, the Yankees put on a thrilling display — Gio Urshela's grand slam, a DJ LeMahieu clutch single, and Gary Sánchez going deep kept the Bronx Bombers alive. But advancing to face the Tampa Bay Rays revealed the ceiling. Gerrit Cole and Masahiro Tanaka were punished for home runs in Games 1 and 2, and Mike Brosseau's gut-punch solo shot off Chapman in the late innings of Game 5 ended the Yankees' season. It was a fitting, painful punctuation on a year that never quite lived up to its promise.",
